  "id": "Compound::DB00563 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Methotrexate is implicated in the development of Breast Cancer through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Methotrexate causes Retinal Vein Thrombosis, which is in turn caused by Anastrozole or Raloxifene, both of which are used to treat Breast Cancer. This creates a chain of causation that links Methotrexate to Breast Cancer via the use of other drugs. The lowest common ancestors of Methotrexate and these downstream drugs are categorized as Enzyme Inhibitors and Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compounds, respectively, highlighting a potential molecular mechanism involving enzyme inhibition or compound class interactions. The evidence also shows that Methotrexate can cause Cranial Nerve Paralysis, which is linked to Paclitaxel, another drug used to treat Breast Cancer, further supporting a broader role of Methotrexate in oncological contexts. The integration of these pathways suggests that Methotrexate may exert its effects on Breast Cancer through a complex network of drug interactions and molecular mechanisms, potentially offering a novel therapeutic avenue.",
